Description: GtkGLArea: the views of an X11 stereo area drawn on their window with OpenGL
 On X11 the two views were read back from the area's framebuffer and put
 into the declared child window through the X server (XPutImage), a copy
 through main memory for every frame. The child window now has an EGL or GLX
 surface of the display's own configuration, and the area's context draws the
 two views on it with a blit and swaps it: nothing leaves the graphics card.
 Measured for the same work (two 1920x1080 views a frame, row 137): about 58
 frames a second at 2.8 ms of processor a frame against 18 at 19 ms on zink,
 60 at 1.7 against 26 to 33 at 16 on NVIDIA's own libraries; on a software
 renderer both ways are slow (12 to 15 frames a second). The OpenGL way is
 the default; the readback stays for a display that gives the window no
 OpenGL surface, said in the log, and behind GDK_X11_STEREO_READBACK=1.
 The first frames are repeated until the window manager has taken the
 declaration (its _KDE_NET_WM_STEREO_DEPTH_LIMITS on the child, version 11 of
 _KDE_NET_WM_STEREO_CONTENT_SUPPORTED) instead of for a fixed second.
 An area that a parent clips (a scrolled window) cannot be a child window of
 its own: it says so with stereo-error and draws its left view alone. A
 stereo-asking area on X11 does not export its picture as a dma-buf (nothing
 takes it there, and GSK's GL renderer shows nothing for such a texture on
 zink).
Author: Daniel Campos Ramos <Capitain_Jack@yahoo.com>
Origin: other, Sparky Stereo OS (queue row 137, milestone 3)
Forwarded: no
Last-Update: 2026-11-25
